"""Cote 16 claim register: authored after reading all 54 supplied scan markers.
This program serializes editorial judgments; it does not infer them from keywords.
"""
import json
from pathlib import Path
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
claims=[]
def add(n,items,scans,lines,words,statement,status,computation,cl,fate,date,present,refs,confidence='high'):
    claims.append(dict(id=f'CL-SA19-{n}',items=[f'SA19-{i}' for i in items],cote=16,scans=scans,lines=lines,his_statement=words,statement=statement,status_on_pages=status,computation=computation,**{'class':cl},fate=fate,fate_date=date,present_status='As of 19 November 2026: '+present,refs=refs,confidence=confidence))
K='Kleiman1968'; G='Grothendieck1969'; S='Stacks2026'
pub68='Publication 1968; a separate first-circulation date for the exact result was not established.'
check='proof_checks.md; scripts/exact_checks.py and outputs/exact_checks.json'
add(201,[],[3,4],'27–122','les relations (1.1) … définissent bien une graduation','A finite complete orthogonal family of idempotents in a unital ring gives a direct block decomposition and a grading by j−i. Conversely degree projections of a finitely graded module satisfy these relations. A subring containing the projections is stable under all homogeneous components.','proved there','Independent block-multiplication proof in proof_checks.md §1; no numerical computation required.','E','Elementary ring decomposition; searched Kleiman 1968 §1 and the intrinsic-grading lemma 3.15 for its use in the correspondence setting. No claim of originality is supported.','Elementary pre-existing algebra; exact earliest reference not established.','Correct with finite support; the projectors are central in E^0, not necessarily E.',[K])
add(202,[124],[5,6,7],'123–233','un idempotent est connu quand on connaît son annulateur','Central idempotents are determined by their annihilator. The two central-annihilator conditions (3.1),(3.2) identify the extreme degree projectors after outer projectors are known. Joint injectivity and spanning images imply these conditions; a two-sided homogeneous inverse implies both.','proved there','Independent proof e=ef=f from equal annihilators; direct verification of both inverse identities, proof_checks.md §1.','B','Matched to the intrinsic-grading argument of Kleiman 1968, Lemma 3.15; the elementary annihilator observation itself predates this application.',pub68,'Correct under the written two-sided conditions; existence of an isomorphism of groups alone does not put its inverse in the chosen subring.',[K])
add(203,[124],[5,8],'123–173;234–264','Trivial si i=n−1','For a finite grading in [0,2n], homogeneous opposite-block inverses in E recover all degree projectors. The displayed assertion that i=n−1 leaves only π_n unknown has the wrong index. The terminal stage is i=n.','proof sketched; false induction-base sentence','Independent two-cutoff induction in proof_checks.md §1; CL-SA19-280 supplies the corrected general argument.','B','Kleiman 1968, Lemma 3.15, provides the later intrinsic-grading construction. The erroneous induction index was not found as a published theorem.',pub68,'The membership theorem is valid with the corrected induction; the source sentence is not silently emended.',[K])
add(204,[101,125],[9,10],'265–349','décomposition primitive','Let M be a module graded in [0,2n], L of degree 2, and assume every L^(n−i):M^i→M^(2n−i), i≤n, is an isomorphism. Then P^i=ker L^(n−i+1) yields M^i=P^i⊕LM^(i−2) and the full Lefschetz string decomposition. This is an integral module assertion only when the isomorphisms are integral.','proved there','Independent splitting proof in proof_checks.md §2.','B','Formal decomposition appears in Kleiman 1968 §1; Deligne 1980 Theorem 4.1.1 proves the ℚ_ℓ hard Lefschetz hypothesis for smooth projective varieties over algebraically closed fields, ℓ invertible. It does not make ordinary integral exterior powers Lefschetz.','Formal account published 1968; ℓ-adic theorem circulated by the Arcata 1974 proceedings sketch (published 1975), full publication 1980; exact first announcement not established.','Valid formal theorem; geometric integral versions require separate torsion and normalization hypotheses.',[K,'Deligne1980','Messing1975'])
add(205,[125],[10,11,12],'306–439','les conditions (i) et (ii) sont équivalentes','Under hard Lefschetz and the finite grading, algebraicity or E-membership of the normalized lowering operator is equivalent to the existence in E of homogeneous inverse maps in all complementary degrees; it entails all primitive and degree projectors in E.','proof sketched','Primitive-decomposition and supported-projector proof, proof_checks.md §§1–2.','B','The exact algebraicity equivalences are developed in Kleiman 1968, Proposition 2.9; this establishes equivalence, not the universal existence of algebraic inverse cycles.',pub68,'Formal equivalence established. Its universal geometric hypothesis is the Lefschetz standard conjecture B, still open in general.',[K,'AnconaEtAl2025'])
add(206,[124,126],[11,12],'350–439','1−Σ π_(i,α′)=Σ π_(i,α′)','For fixed degree i, remove the already constructed larger string exponents and use L^α w_(i−2α)L^(n−i+α) on the remaining supported summand. Globally q must be π_i−Σ_(α′>α)π_(i,α′); using 1 gives the stated equality only on M^i.','proof sketched; global support convention unstated','Independent degree-support check in proof_checks.md §2; compare CL-SA19-280.','B','The supported primitive-projector formalism is in Kleiman 1968 §§1–2. A literal global equality with 1 was not corroborated; searched the published formulas and inverse arguments.',pub68,'Correct after explicitly restricting to M^i or inserting π_i. Unrestricted extension of w outside its prescribed block cannot be ignored.',[K])
add(207,[126],[13,14,33],'440–527;1246–1316','ΛL=1−p_top; LΛ=1−p_bottom','On a Lefschetz string L^j p, normalized Λ lowers j by one for j>0 and kills its bottom. Its degree is −2. The endpoint identities and the matrix-unit relations follow with the appropriate bottom and top projectors. The unfinished (6.5) expression is not a definition.','proof sketched; some formulas cancelled or unfinished',check+'; normalized string checks for n≤5 and all-n argument in proof_checks.md §§2–3.','B','Matched to Kleiman 1968 §1 and Proposition 2.9. Standard sl₂ contraction with j(n−i−j+1) coefficients is a distinct normalization.',pub68,'Correct for unit-step lowering. The written degree +2 on scan 13 and literal ordinary contraction on scan 14 are incompatible with it.',[K])
add(208,[101],[14],'482–527','sur ℤ ! … satisfait à la condition de (6.1)','The proposed ordinary exterior algebra over ℤ with ξ=Σe_i∧f_i and ordinary symplectic contraction satisfies the draft’s integral Lefschetz isomorphisms and unit-step endpoint relations.','false as stated',check+'; ξ^n=n!vol checked n=1,…,5; DL^j1=j(n−j+1)L^(j−1)1.','D','Searched Kleiman’s formal conventions, the standard-conjecture literature and the exact exterior normalization. No later theorem validates the literal integral assertion; rationalization repairs only the factorial obstruction. See CL-SA19-279 for the explicitly reconstructed replacement.','No valid literal later result identified; countercheck in this run 2026.','False for n≥2 over ℤ; ordinary contraction remains wrongly normalized even over ℚ. A different lattice or operator must be stated.',[K])
add(209,[123],[15],'528–597','0≤α,β≤2n−i','The proposed matrix units on the string starting in degree i use exponents α,β up to 2n−i. In the normalized model the correct range is 0≤α,β≤n−i, with product of matrix algebras of sizes n−i+1.','false as stated in the displayed range',check+'; matrix-unit check n=0,…,5 and general proof in §3.','D','Searched the normalized Lefschetz-algebra formalism in Kleiman 1968; no source confirms the printed overlong range. The second pass confirms the ink, so this remains a mathematical correction.','2026 countercheck; no exact published repair identified.','Corrected range gives rank Σ_(r=1)^(n+1)r²=(n+1)(n+2)(2n+3)/6; see CL-SA19-279.',[K])
add(210,[122],[15,16],'528–649','l’hom. annoncé … est unique','After replacing the defective exterior model by the normalized integral string algebra Φ_n, a homomorphism preserving L and the prescribed grading projectors is determined by the unit-step lowering and primitive decomposition; the proposed equivalence is conditional on a valid definition of Φ_n.','proof sketched','All-n matrix-unit construction and uniqueness analysis, proof_checks.md §3.','C','Kleiman 1968 develops the formal Lefschetz operators. No exact identification of the literal integral algebra on scan 14 with the normalized order was found in the searched literature.','Formal analogues published 1968; exact integral manuscript model unresolved.','The reconstructed universal model is valid. Its identity with the manuscript’s literal exterior-contraction algebra is not established.',[K])
add(211,[102],[16,17],'598–692','condition essentielle ! … Non','Prescribing only the raising element L does not determine a homomorphism from the Lefschetz algebra. Already Φ_1=Mat₂(ℤ)×ℤ has automorphisms Ad(1+tL) fixing L and changing Λ and the grading.','corrected by himself later on scan 17',check+'; exact n=1 conjugation; rank five.','D','The draft itself rejects the stronger uniqueness claim. Searched later formal accounts and did not identify a separate publication of this exact counterexample; no open uniqueness question remains.','Negative answer already on these undated pages; explicit conjugation supplied by the earlier run and rechecked in 2026.','False without the grading data; true only in the explicitly prescribed graded setting of CL-SA19-210.',[K])
add(212,[102,124],[17],'650–692','(w_i L^(n−i)−1)π_(2n−i)=0','The displayed criterion uses the upper projector after a degree-zero composite whose inverse identity belongs on the lower degree. The correct pair is (w_iL^(n−i)−1)π_i=0 and (L^(n−i)w_i−1)π_(2n−i)=0.','false as stated',check+'; n=1 returns −π₂ on the literal left-hand side.','D','The two-sided identities are already correctly written on scan 7 and in Kleiman’s formal setup. The exact erroneous projector is confirmed by second pass, not repaired as transcription.','Earlier correct formulation on scan 7; publication analogue 1968.','Literal formula refuted; use CL-SA19-280 and the two identities, with neither omitted over a general ring.',[K])
add(213,[],[18],'693–724','principe de dualité … L^n≠0','In an already Lefschetz graded correspondence ring, passing to the opposite ring and reversing degrees exchanges L with Λ. Nonredundancy of the degree interval is equivalently detected by π_0, π_(2n), E^(±2n), and L^n,Λ^n being nonzero. This is not a criterion making an arbitrary element Lefschetz.','proof sketched','Endpoint inverse identities prove each nonvanishing equivalence; independent verification in proof_checks.md §§3–4.','B','Formal duality is part of Kleiman 1968 §1. The proposed converse is separately rejected on scan 22.','Publication analogue 1968.','Correct for an existing Lefschetz structure; counterexample CL-SA19-215 prevents reversing the implication for arbitrary L′.',[K])
add(214,[],[20,22],'780–859;898–944','Λ=Ω(L)Δ(L)^(−N)','Scaling an existing raising operator by an invertible scalar scales normalized lowering inversely. The more ambitious rational-expression sketch Ω(L)Δ(L)^(−N) leaves Ω,Δ,N and its parameter space undefined. For two existing Lefschetz operators with rank-one extreme blocks, their top-power comparison coefficients are reciprocal units.','stated without proof; rational formula unfinished','Scaling follows on each string; rank-one αβ=1 checked algebraically in proof_checks.md §4.','F','Searched the polynomial dependence discussions and standard-conjecture formalism in Kleiman 1968; no uniquely identifiable theorem matches the undefined Ω,Δ sketch.','No exact fate assignable to the incomplete expression.','The scaling and rank-one comparison are valid under stated hypotheses. The rational-expression proposal remains unidentified, not an established formula.',[K],'medium')
add(215,[],[20,22],'780–859;898–944','L est Lefschetzien, L′ ne l’est pas, bien que L′³=L³ !','In R=k[L,L′]/(L⁴,(L′)²−LL′,L²L′−L³,L³L′), both generators have degree 2. The six displayed normal forms form a basis. L is Lefschetz, L′ has singular middle multiplication, and (L′)³=L³≠0.','proof sketched; counterexample supplied by himself',check+'; two exact commuting 6×6 matrices verify all relations, middle ranks 2 and 1, common nonzero cube.','D','Targeted searches of Lefschetz graded-algebra counterexamples and Kleiman’s formal accounts did not locate publication of this exact presentation. Its correctness is established directly here.','Undated source counterexample; exact verification 2026.','Correct over every field. Nonzero top power is insufficient for all Lefschetz isomorphisms.',[K])
add(216,[103,109,111],[19],'725–779','NS(U) … conoyau … canoniquement autodual','Under the local-surface resolution hypotheses of the surrounding Brauer text, NS(U) is the finite cokernel of the exceptional intersection matrix Q. For any nonsingular symmetric integral Q, D_Q has perfect symmetric pairing xᵀQ⁻¹y modulo ℤ. The isolated page does not itself specify all geometric hypotheses.','stated without proof; geometric context omitted from reused sheet','Independent lattice proof in proof_checks.md §6; Q=(−m) gives −ab/m.','A','Matched by title/numbering to Grothendieck, Brauer III §8; Mumford 1961 proves negative definiteness and the topological cokernel calculation in the complex analytic normal surface setting. Exact Brauer publication passage awaited direct reading in research log.','Brauer III circulated as IHÉS manuscript March 1966; published 1968. Mumford received May 1960, published 1961.','Lattice assertion proved. Geometric identification requires the omitted local-surface hypotheses; no extension from Mumford to all characteristics is inferred.', ['Grothendieck1968BrauerIII','Mumford1961'],'medium')
add(217,[104],[19],'725–779','indépendante de la résolution X′ choisie','The discriminant pairing is unchanged when the exceptional lattice is enlarged by a point blowup: its new matrix is integrally congruent to Q⊕(−1). Independence between resolutions additionally requires common domination by such blowups in the relevant surface category.','stated without proof',check+'; symbolic block congruence and all-rank proof in proof_checks.md §6.','A','Grothendieck’s Brauer III §8 is the exact publication lead; Mumford’s lattice/topological proof supplies the earlier geometric context. No claim that blowup invariance alone identifies the étale pairing.','Own published context 1968; earlier complex-analytic context 1961.','Correct for the lattice pairing under the stated factorization condition.', ['Grothendieck1968BrauerIII','Mumford1961'],'medium')
add(218,[109],[19],'725–779','Pic(U)_0 est un groupe ℓ-divisible','For a smooth connected commutative algebraic group over an algebraically closed field k and ℓ≠char k, multiplication by ℓ is surjective on k-points. The stated Picard realization therefore gives Pic(U)^0 ℓ-divisible and Pic(U)/ℓ^r≅NS(U)/ℓ^r.','stated without proof; cited group-theoretic input','Proof uses surjectivity of multiplication on connected commutative groups; no numerical computation.','A','Searched the explicit Brauer III §8.4–8.5 and SGA group-scheme context. The source itself attributes the commutative-group input to [9, exp.4], whose bibliography is not the unrelated scan-28 list.','Brauer text circulated 1966, published 1968; group-theoretic input earlier.','Valid with algebraically closed k, smooth connected group and ℓ invertible; not a statement about all Picard groups over arbitrary fields.', ['Grothendieck1968BrauerIII'],'medium')
add(219,[106],[21,23],'860–897;945–977','t^(i−1)→H^i(X,ℤ_ℓ[1])→T_ℓ(H^i(X,μ_(ℓ∞)))','Under the finiteness and inverse-limit hypotheses of Brauer III §8.1, ℓ-adic cohomology is an extension of the finite nondivisible quotient of preceding torsion cohomology by the Tate module in degree i. The quotient of torsion cohomology by its divisible part is the torsion of the next ℓ-adic group.','proof sketched by passage to limits','Exact-sequence degree shift checked; no computation with finite samples would establish the required limit hypotheses.','A','Exact numbering (8.3)–(8.6) identifies Brauer III §8.1. Later Kummer/Brauer references were searched for the same degree shift, not merely the broad subject.','Circulated March 1966; published 1968.','Correct with finiteness and ℓ-primary interpretation. A Tate module records the divisible part and does not recover the finite nondivisible quotient.', ['Grothendieck1968BrauerIII'],'medium')
add(220,[106],[21],'860–897','0→NS(X)⊗ℤ_ℓ→H²(X,ℤ_ℓ[1])→T_ℓ Br′(X)→0','For ℓ invertible on X, assume Pic(X) is an extension of a finitely generated NS(X) by a divisible group and the stated finite-cohomology hypotheses. Passing to inverse limits in Kummer yields the displayed exact sequence; the ℓ-primary torsion of NS identifies with the torsion of H².','proof sketched','Kummer limit argument and distinction between NS⊗ℤ_ℓ and Pic⊗ℚ_ℓ/ℤ_ℓ checked; no numerical computation.','A','Own later Brauer III §8.2, equation (8.7), is the exact publication match; the source explicitly cites Brauer II §3.1. Crosslink to c43/132–133 must retain the surface/general-X hypotheses.','Circulated March 1966; published 1968.','Established with those hypotheses. It does not force T_ℓ Br′=0 for surfaces merely because a curve case vanishes.', ['Grothendieck1968BrauerIII'],'medium')
add(221,[106],[21,23],'860–897;945–977','s’identifie canoniquement à l’orthogonal … forme quadratique non dégénérée','For a smooth proper connected surface over algebraically closed k, ℓ≠char k, the rational Brauer Tate module identifies with the orthogonal complement of NS⊗ℚ_ℓ in H²(ℚ_ℓ(1)). Under the unimodular free cohomology pairing and saturated NS inclusion, its induced lattice form is integral exactly when the NS discriminant is an ℓ-adic unit, then self-dual. The separate finite Brauer quotient is an ℓ-primary H³ torsion group.','proof sketched','Lattice splitting and inverse-discriminant argument; prime-primary qualification of (8.8),(8.9) checked.','A','Exact match sought in Brauer III §§8.2–8.3. The intersection nondegeneracy is attributed on the page to Matsusaka [27], not Tate [27] from scan 28.','Circulated 1966; published 1968.','Rational pairing and finite quotient are distinct. As written without ℓ-primary notation, (8.8)–(8.9) cannot identify the entire Brauer group’s nondivisible quotient with a single ℓ-adic group.', ['Grothendieck1968BrauerIII'],'medium')
add(222,[110],[19,23],'725–779;945–977','autodualité symétrique … relations de dualité','Do the local discriminant pairing and the pairing coming from étale cohomological duality agree under the geometric identifications, including twists and signs? This is the earlier notes’ precise compatibility question; the source sheets themselves give two constructions but no comparison proof.','open on the pages / comparison not proved on these sheets','No purported proof: elementary lattice checks do not identify the étale construction.','C','The earlier run points to Brauer III Remark 8.5. The exact remark was sought in the published text and backward literature; until direct text comparison is obtained its alleged resolution is not treated as checked.','Brauer III published 1968; exact historical comparison status remains unverified.','Separate geometrically meaningful comparison; do not mark solved by Mumford or by the elementary inverse-matrix formula alone.', ['Grothendieck1968BrauerIII','Mumford1961'],'low')
add(223,[138],[25],'978–1062','Condition d’associativité … [boxed identity]','Over a field of characteristic different from 2, the specified graded-commutative law on k,V,kξ⊕W,V∨,kξ² is associative exactly when the displayed four-variable identity relating φ and Q∘ψ holds. Nondegeneracy of φ and Q alone does not imply that identity.','proof sketched',check+'; V=k² symplectic,W=0 violates the identity at x=t=e,y=z=f; reduction of associativity by degree in proof_checks.md §7.','D','Searched Hodge/Lefschetz algebra descriptions, alternating four-form realizations and standard-conjecture formal accounts. No exact later publication of this parameterization was identified; direct algebra verifies it.','Undated manuscript; exact audit 2026.','Valid parameterization with the separate associativity equation. The counterexample is the earlier run’s warning, not a refutation of a claim that Grothendieck made.',[K])
add(224,[138],[25],'978–1062','Condition de Lefschetz … φ non dégénérée; Condition de Poincaré … Q non dégénérée','In the same graded law, multiplication by ξ:H¹→H³ is φ♭ and ξ²:H⁰→H⁴ is identity. Thus Lefschetz is equivalent to nondegenerate φ. Poincaré duality is equivalent to nondegenerate Q because the middle pairing is 1⊕Q and H¹×H³ is evaluation. Associativity remains separately required to obtain an algebra.','proved there','Direct matrices of the pairings, proof_checks.md §7; no extra numerical computation.','D','Exact construction searched with the four-form and formal Lefschetz literature; no matched publication found.','No later exact fate identified.','Correct with stated characteristic/conventions and separate associativity.',[K])
add(225,[139],[27],'1113–1155','une droite isotrope D … alors φ sera aussi non isotrope','Over an algebraically closed field of characteristic ≠2, with nondegenerate Q on nonzero W, an isotropic line D⊂kξ⊕W not lying in W permits α to be a nondegenerate alternating form valued in D. The induced φ is nondegenerate and the four-form χ vanishes.','construction said to work',check+'; explicit diag(1,−1), ξ=(1,0), D=k(1,1), α=φ(1,1).','D','Searched alternating-form/Hodge-algebra construction and Kleiman’s framework; no exact published example located. The construction is independently verified.','No exact later publication identified.','Valid. The ink says non isotrope where nondegenerate is the intended mathematical property; no scan decision is made.',[K])
add(226,[139,140],[27,29],'1113–1206','une extension de la forme χ′','An alternating four-form χ on V induces a symmetric pairing χ′ on Λ²V. A form-preserving map α into a symmetric space (W̃,Q), together with Q(ξ,ξ)=1, reconstructs the associative graded algebra; nondegenerate Q and φ(x,y)=Q(α(x∧y),ξ) impose Poincaré and Lefschetz.','construction said to work','Direct reduction to the scan-25 identity; proof_checks.md §7.','D','Targeted searches of four-forms, graded Poincaré algebras and standard-conjecture models found no exact later realization theorem for this manuscript parameterization.','No exact historical fate established.','Valid algebraic reconstruction with the stated form-preserving condition and characteristic convention; it does not realize the algebra as the cohomology of a variety.',[K])
add(227,[140],[29],'1188–1206','un choix convenable de ξ … n’importe quelle φ … au facteur scalaire','If χ′ is nondegenerate, every alternating functional φ has a unique inverse image ξ under Q♭. Requiring Q(ξ,ξ)=1 further requires a nonzero norm and a suitable square class, even when φ is nondegenerate. The unqualified up-to-scalar assertion does not supply those conditions.','false as stated without norm/field qualifications','Norm obstruction derived in proof_checks.md §7; no geometric counterexample claimed.','D','Searched the exact four-form reconstruction and orbit question; no later source removes the elementary norm obstruction.','2026 clarification; no exact later repair identified.','Use only the norm-qualified reconstruction. Algebraic closedness removes a nonzero square-class obstruction but not isotropic inverse images.',[K])
add(228,[140],[29],'1188–1206','l’orbite … pourront être infinies, si dim V≥4 (ou 6?)','For the endomorphism u formed from the inverse alternating form and χ′, can ξ and φ̌ have infinite forward orbits? The tensor-square convention and the 4-versus-6 threshold are not fixed clearly enough to identify the literal u uniquely. Under the earlier run’s convention U=(Λ²φ♯)Q♭ there is a verified dimension-six example.','open on the pages',check+'; full 15×15 Q, norm=1, det(Q)=2097152/59049, positive-growth argument.','C','Exact phrase/formula searches through Hodge algebra, alternating four-form and Lefschetz literature found no exact published resolution. The earlier run’s example is rechecked here, not attributed to literature or to the manuscript.','No exact published fate identified; conditional example already proposed by earlier run, independently checked 2026.','Conditional dimension-six answer proved for the specified normalization. Literal source operator and sharp threshold remain undecided.',[K],'medium')
add(229,[140],[29],'1188–1206','linéairement engendrées … de dim non bornée','Can the linear spans of the indicated orbits have unbounded dimension as dim V increases? For the earlier normalized operator, the supplied block-matrix family has arbitrarily large cyclic rank, nondegenerate four-form pairing and normalized ξ.','open on the pages',check+'; m=2,3,4 exact tests plus all-m Vandermonde/open-condition argument in proof_checks.md §7.','C','Targeted searches did not identify this exact family or conjecture in later publications. The family comes from the earlier notes; this run checks it without developing a new research direction.','Earlier-run construction; all-dimension proof checked 2026.','Affirmative for the explicitly normalized model. No claim of literature novelty, geometric realization or resolution of an ambiguous ink normalization.',[K],'medium')

add(230,[118],[26],'1063–1112','C_η se prolonge en un sous-groupe de Cartan C de G','In the finite-presentation group-scheme setting, a generic Cartan subgroup, after the reductions stated in SGA3 XV §6, extends over a dense open, and an inclusion into H spreads after further shrinking. This is an editorial correction to an existing proof, not a stand-alone theorem without hypotheses.','stated as correction to another text','Direct comparison with revised SGA3 XV, proof of Proposition 6.2(iii), reprint p.273 (original p.427–428); no independent full proof claimed.','A','Exact corrected passage found in SGA3 XV Proposition 6.2(iii). The reprint says C_η is Cartan in H_η at that step; surrounding centralizer reduction connects it to G. XV–XVII were written by M. Raynaud from Grothendieck’s succinct 1964 notes, according to their own introduction.','Underlying notes 1964; original SGA3 II publication 1970; modern reprint checked.','Published argument identified with its context. Other page-number corrections are edition-level material, not new mathematical claims.', ['SGA3II1970'])
add(231,[120],[28,30],'1156–1187;1207–1237','H^r(A,G)=0 for r>2','The reused English sheet states cohomological vanishing above degree 2 and reduces it using Thms 1,4 and Prop.14, none present. The topology, base A, field k and class of G are not sufficiently supplied to state the exact theorem. Its author is not established by the sheet.','proof sketched in a reused typescript fragment','No computation; logical dependency on missing hypotheses recorded.','F','Searched Shatz’s publications, Annals 1964/1966, exact English phrases, the Tate 1959 and Rosenberg–Zelinsky 1962 backward citations. Shatz 1966 Cohomological dimension in Grothendieck topologies is a lead, not an identification.','Cited Shatz paper marked to appear (published 1964); the fragment’s date and exact publication remain unestablished.','Unidentified exact statement; do not attribute it to Grothendieck or extend to arbitrary local rings/group schemes.', ['Shatz1964','Shatz1966','RosenbergZelinsky1962','Tate1959'],'low')
add(232,[120],[28,30],'1156–1187;1207–1237','For each s>0, the complex C(s) is acyclic','For a purely inseparable extension K/k, the alternating face-map complex formed from H^s(K^⊗r,G_m) is asserted acyclic for s>0. The cohomology topology and preceding theorem supplying the comparison are absent from the reused sheet.','stated without proof on the supplied page','No computation claimed; the complex and all tensor products are recorded.','F','Followed the explicit backward reference Rosenberg–Zelinsky, Amitsur’s complex for inseparable fields (1962), and searched Shatz’s Grothendieck-topology papers and exact Proposition 15 wording. No uniquely verified publication match to this fragment.','Backward source 1962; exact fragment publication not identified.','Plausible with the intended flat/Grothendieck cohomology conventions; no unrestricted theorem or authorship is inferred.', ['RosenbergZelinsky1962','Shatz1966'],'low')
add(233,[127],[34],'1317–1364','sur les composantes primitives','For a smooth hyperplane Y⊂X, dim X=n, weak and hard Lefschetz plus Poincaré duality give restriction ρ and Gysin γ with γρ=L_X and ργ=L_Y. Each nontrivial X-primitive string restricts to a string one term shorter; Y may have an additional primitive middle summand killed by γ.','proof sketched','All-degree string proof and exact model checks, proof_checks.md §5.','B','The same hyperplane formalism is developed in Kleiman 1968 Proposition 2.12 and Theorem 2.13, with standard weak/hard Lefschetz inputs.',pub68,'Correct formal geometry under these hypotheses; an algebraic inverse of L remains a separate standard-conjecture assumption.',[K,'Deligne1980'])
add(234,[127],[36],'1393–1445','Im φ_* … Ker φ_*','With the preceding smooth-hyperplane hypotheses, ker γ is the additional middle primitive part of Y, and im γ is the sum of all X-string terms except primitive bottoms. Restrictions, transposes and orthogonal complements in the manuscript must be read with their displayed domains.','proof sketched','Explicit string kernels and images, proof_checks.md §5.','B','Matched to Kleiman 1968 Proposition 2.12 and the proof of Theorem 2.13; the support restrictions are required for the published splitting formulas.',pub68,'Correct with the geometric weak-Lefschetz decomposition; no unqualified injectivity/surjectivity in every degree.',[K])
add(235,[126],[33],'1246–1316','ΛL=… ; LΛ=…','The endpoint projector formulary characterizes normalized raising/lowering on primitive strings. Cancelled alternatives do not replace the surviving endpoint formulas. Every projector in a formula acts on the displayed degree, or must be extended by zero explicitly.','proved there / formulas revised on same page','Exact string identities and endpoint checks; proof_checks.md §§2,5.','B','Kleiman 1968 §1 and Proposition 2.9 provide the matched later formalism.',pub68,'Valid under unit-step normalization; scan alarms list degree/index variants separately.',[K])
add(236,[127],[38],'1464–1522','coïncide … i+j=n−1','Restriction and lowering agree on the shared part of a primitive string, with the original top endpoint excluded. The displayed condition at the affected comparison is equality i+j=n−1, not the earlier notes’ weak inequality. The bottom formula contains maps whose literal variance is inconsistent.','proof sketched; formula needs support/variance clarification',check+'; top-endpoint failure explicitly checked.','B','The exact formal setting is matched to Kleiman 1968 Proposition 2.12; the manuscript’s pullback/Gysin symbols still require ink review.','Publication analogue 1968; second-pass current reading retained.','Use the supported string formulas; do not silently replace two pullbacks by Gysin maps or change the confirmed equality.',[K])
add(237,[127],[40],'1544–1585','Λ_Y=φ^*Λ_X²φ_*','For ρ:Y-restriction and γ:Gysin, normalized lowering satisfies Λ_Y=ρΛ_X²γ. With q=γΛ_Yρ, q is the projector onto strict interiors of X-strings and γΛ_Y^mρ=qΛ_X^(m−1)q for m≥1. If T=1+q, Λ_XL_X+L_XΛ_X=T−p_n, with the length-one middle projector p_n.','proof sketched',check+'; every string length 1–8 and endpoint m checked; general basis proof §5.','B','Matched to the hyperplane reduction in Kleiman 1968 §§2.12–2.13; the explicit compressed identities are also independently proved here.',pub68,'Correct in the normalized setup. In particular B(X) implies B(Y).',[K])
add(238,[127],[40],'1544–1585','[projector transfer] i+j<n−1','The transfer of a non-top primitive-string projector from Y to X uses γ p_(Y,j) Λ_Y ρ=p_(X,j+1) only below the strict boundary j≤r−2 on an X-string of length r+1. At j=r−1 the left side is zero but the top projector is not.','proof sketched',check+'; strict-boundary countertest and all-rank proof §5.','B','Searched and compared Kleiman’s hyperplane splitting formulas; the strict condition agrees with string degree counting. The second-pass inclusion of the leading Λ is essential.','1968 formal publication; exact boundary checked 2026.','Correct with strict inequality and the specified lowering factor; a non-strict extension is false.',[K])
add(239,[127],[42],'1614–1705','[algebraicity via hyperplane and a product]','An algebraic splitting of the critical hyperplane map, combined with algebraic lowering on the hyperplane, supplies lowering on X. The required product-cycle condition produces that splitting. The argument is not B(Y)⇒B(X) without an added cycle condition.','proof sketched','Logical reconstruction using γρ=L and supported inverses; proof_checks.md §5.','B','Exact later theorem is Kleiman 1968 Theorem 2.13, which keeps B(Y) and the critical algebraic-cycle Lefschetz condition on X×Y (or X×X).',pub68,'Established conditional reduction; it does not settle B(X) for an arbitrary variety.',[K])
add(240,[127],[45,46,50],'1728–1784;1833–1860','C(X) … C(Y)+A(X×X)^0','For a successive smooth hyperplane flag, the critical algebraic-cycle Lefschetz conditions on suitable products together with B on the next hyperplane imply B(X). Quantifying the cycle conjecture A over all smooth projective varieties yields its equivalence with universal B. A(X) for one fixed X is insufficient for that deduction. Draft C denotes inverse-Lefschetz algebraicity and corresponds to later B.','proof sketched','Quantifiers and product requirements checked against source and Kleiman; no finite numerical computation.','B','Kleiman 1968 Theorem 2.13 and Theorem 3.16 develop the exact flag/product and universal implications.',pub68,'Established as a conditional/universal equivalence. Individual varieties and universal fields-of-definition assertions are kept distinct.',[K])
add(241,[127],[45,46],'1728–1784','[produits, spécialisation, changement de polarisation]','The draft lists stability of the inverse-Lefschetz condition under products, smooth hyperplane restriction, change of polarization and suitable specialization. Each is conditional on the ambient Weil cohomology, algebraicity and base-change framework; unrestricted deformation invariance is not supplied.','stated without proof / sketched in letter','The product and hyperplane formal algebra is checked; specialization is carried with its needed smooth-proper hypotheses, not independently reproved.','B','Kleiman 1968 Proposition 2.10 treats polarization independence, Proposition 2.12 the hyperplane operation, and the later standard-conjecture formalism treats the indicated stability. Exact unrestricted specialization statement not identified from the draft.','1968 formal results; no single date assigned to an unspecified specialization claim.','Product/polarization/hyperplane results established in their stated categories; blanket invariance over arbitrary singular families is unsupported.',[K],'medium')
add(242,[131],[44],'1717–1727','en caractéristique zéro … algèbre de dimension finie sur Q','For smooth projective complex varieties, Hodge theory supplies hard Lefschetz and the Hodge-index positivity on algebraic primitive classes; homological correspondence classes form a finite-dimensional ℚ-space in rational Betti cohomology. Over ℚ_ℓ, finite-dimensionality over ℚ does not follow from embedding in End_(ℚ_ℓ) alone; positivity plus rational trace Gram matrices supplies the needed bound.','stated without proof','Independent Gram-matrix proof in proof_checks.md §8.','B','Kleiman 1968 §3, especially Lemma 3.13, explains the rational finite-dimensionality/positivity argument. No blanket statement that the standard conjecture A holds in characteristic zero is inferred.','Hodge-theoretic input earlier; algebraic formalization published 1968.','Correct qualified statements; hard Lefschetz and positivity do not automatically make every inverse algebraic.',[K])
add(243,[130,133],[44,46],'1717–1727;1759–1784','[cohomology-independent motives] … [withdrawn in positive characteristic]','The draft’s unconditional claim that correspondence constructions are independent of the chosen cohomology theory in positive characteristic is withdrawn/qualified in the same letter. A later conditional theorem assumes numerical=homological equivalence and positivity in the relevant products.','corrected by himself later','Logical audit of the cancellation and hypotheses; dual-number example shows nilpotent kernel need not be zero.','C','Kleiman 1968 Theorem 3.16 gives conditional independence. Jannsen 1992 proves semisimplicity of numerical motives and identifies a nilpotent radical under additional Künneth hypotheses; it does not prove the general equivalence of numerical and homological motives.','Conditional account 1968; Jannsen submitted 12 July 1991, published 1992.','General cohomology independence is not established by the numerical semisimplicity theorem.',[K,'Jannsen1992'])
add(244,[135],[46,47],'1759–1796','known in dimensions 1 and 2','The low-dimensional inverse-Lefschetz assertion uses curves/Picard–Albanese to construct the H¹ inverse for surfaces; top and bottom degrees are elementary. This low-dimensional list is in the first Coates letter, dated4.1.67 on scan44. The separate second letter begins on scan48 and reads6.1.1966; its date is not transferred to this list.','stated as known; historical dating issue','Compared the exact Picard/Albanese proof in Kleiman 1968 2A9–2A10; no new proof of Picard theory claimed.','B','Kleiman 1968 Propositions 2A9–2A10 prove the curve/surface statement for Weil cohomologies satisfying the displayed H¹/Picard dimension condition, including ℓ-adic cohomology.','Publication1968; this list belongs to the first letter dated4January1967. The separate second letter dated6January1966 is preserved literally, without chronological amendment.','Known low-dimensional case; the date and intended correspondence chronology remain documentary questions.',[K])
add(245,[136],[47],'1785–1796','Hodge (1938) … π_i','The letter credits Hodge’s 1938 paper with surface Künneth projectors and worries that the result was neglected. The mathematical symbol is π_i under the second pass, not π_1. A reception-history assertion requires evidence beyond the letter.','stated historical attribution','Bibliographic comparison with Hodge 1938 and Kleiman 1968 introduction; no reception-history proof.','E','Hodge’s Algebraic correspondences between surfaces matches the supplied title, volume and opening page. Publisher access failed here; Kleiman 1968 explicitly credits the earlier surface decomposition. The strong claim of neglect was not independently substantiated.','Hodge publication 1938; letter date as above.','Earlier surface attribution identified; full Hodge article and historical reception are not independently verified here.', ['Hodge1938',K],'medium')
add(246,[136],[47],'1785–1796','this cannot work … X would have to be simply connected','The proposed universal birational/smooth-specialization reduction to nonsingular hypersurfaces fails: for dimension at least two, smooth projective hypersurfaces are simply connected; in the appropriate smooth proper setting specialization and birational invariance preserve the obstruction. A variety with nontrivial fundamental group cannot be obtained by that proposed chain. A clean reconstruction uses complex smooth projective varieties, smooth proper families and ordinary π₁.','corrected by himself, reporting Serre’s objection','Checked the exact chain stated on the page and the fundamental-group hypotheses; no claim about arbitrary singular degeneration.','B','SGA1 exposés X and XII supply the étale specialization and Lefschetz/comparison framework. The letter itself rejects the suggested reduction; the separate Serre 1958 bibliography on scan 28 is not evidence for this allusion.','Objection reported in the January letter; underlying SGA1 lectures 1960–1961, publication 1971.','Correct qualified obstruction. Extending the standard-conjecture program to singular varieties is left as a new task, not solved by this remark.', ['SGA1'],'medium')
add(247,[129],[48,49],'1797–1832','Hamilton-Cayley … w⁻¹u=v⁻¹','Assume hard Lefschetz, rational characteristic polynomials for algebraic endomorphisms, and algebraic isomorphisms u:H^(2n−i)→H^i. For v=L^(n−i), w=uv has nonzero constant characteristic coefficient; Cayley–Hamilton makes w⁻¹ and then v⁻¹=w⁻¹u algebraic. Thus the algebraic-isomorphism condition implies inverse-Lefschetz algebraicity under the coefficient hypothesis.','proved there',check+'; symbolic 2×2 identity plus all-dimension Cayley–Hamilton proof §8.','B','Exact theorem and proof are Kleiman 1968 Proposition 2.9. The draft’s C is later B; rationality of coefficients is a genuine premise.','Publication 1968; draft writes 6.1.1966.','Correct after resolving the displayed u/w polynomial notation mathematically; no ink change made.',[K])
add(248,[129,132],[49],'1819–1832','Then u⁻¹ is algebraic','If X and X′ satisfy inverse-Lefschetz algebraicity and the rational-characteristic-polynomial hypotheses used above, an algebraic isomorphism between indicated cohomology degrees has algebraic inverse: identify algebraically with duals, form u′u, and apply Cayley–Hamilton.','proved there','Independent algebraic argument in proof_checks.md §8.','B','Matched exactly to Kleiman 1968 Proposition 2.11 and its transpose/inverse proof.',pub68,'Established conditional result. Algebraic monomorphisms and epimorphisms require the additional positivity/splitting argument.',[K])
add(249,[131],[49],'1819–1832','C(X) … I(X×X) … correspondance classes … semi-simple','Assume the algebraic Lefschetz involution and the positive trace form supplied by the appropriate Hodge index conjecture on X×X. The finite-dimensional algebra of correspondence classes is semisimple because its involution-stable radical has zero trace norm.','hope / proof requested in letter','Independent radical proof and ℚ-dimension Gram bound, proof_checks.md §8.','B','Kleiman 1968 Theorem 3.12 and Lemma 3.13 establish this conditional statement. Jannsen’s 1992 unconditional numerical theorem is a different quotient statement.','Kleiman publication 1968; Jannsen publication 1992.','Conditional homological semisimplicity established; universal positivity/numerical=homological remain separate conjectures.',[K,'Jannsen1992'])
add(250,[132],[49],'1819–1832','there should exist a left-inverse which is algebraic','Under algebraic adjoints and positive nondegenerate forms in the full correspondence setting of the source and target, algebraic injections split algebraically by (u* u)⁻¹u*, and surjections by the dual formula. Algebraic image membership then pulls back along an injection.','open step with proof idea','Independent adjoint formula; hypotheses on ambient category made explicit in proof_checks.md §8.','B','Kleiman 1968 Theorem 3.14 develops the requested splitting result after the positivity/semisimplicity lemmas.','Publication 1968.','Established under the appropriate simultaneous hypotheses; I(X×X) alone is not silently assumed to control arbitrary unrelated X′.',[K])
add(251,[128],[50],'1833–1860','week Lefschets relations … A′, A″','The two weak cycle-lifting conditions, restricted to critical degrees and to successive products along a hyperplane flag, are claimed to imply the full algebraic-cycle Lefschetz condition. Literal formulas mix H^i with inequalities in 2i and contain uncertain A/C and j/i; their exact manuscript formulation is not fixed.','stated with proof sketch; degree conventions ambiguous','Dimension and variance audit; no silently repaired numerical formula.','C','Kleiman 1968 Theorem 2.13 supplies the precise nearby product/critical-degree theorem. It is not evidence that each literal A′/A″ formula on this page is correctly transcribed.','Publication of related precise theorem 1968.','A defensible reconstructed theorem is established in the literature; the source’s exact weak-variant equivalence remains scan-dependent.',[K],'medium')
add(252,[121],[50],'1833–1860','deduction of the Weil conjectures from the conjectures C and A','The letter asks for a deduction of the Weil conjectures from its conjectures C and A. The precise positivity content of these draft letters is not defined on the supplied page. The standard route to the Riemann-hypothesis estimate uses algebraic Lefschetz adjoints plus positive trace form; algebraicity alone is insufficient.','open programmatic step','Logical dependency audit; no numerical verification of Weil bounds asserted.','C','Kleiman 1968 Theorem 4.7 gives a precise conditional result with B(X) and I(X×X). Deligne later proved the Weil conjectures by a different route; this does not prove the full standard conjectures.','Conditional argument publication 1968; Deligne Weil I 1974 and Weil II 1980.','Historical implication developed with explicit positivity. Undefined draft A cannot be replaced by later A without a definition.',[K,'Deligne1980'])
add(253,[124],[51],'1861–1882','A contient … les projections sur les facteurs élémentaires','Let A⊂End H* be stable under homogeneous components and transposition and contain the raising operator ξ. Assume homogeneous two-sided inverses on all complementary degree blocks. Then all degree and primitive-string projectors and normalized lowering lie in A.','proof sketched across scans 51,53,55','Corrected two-sided support proof in proof_checks.md §§1–2; CL-SA19-280.','B','Kleiman 1968 Proposition 2.9 and Lemma 3.15 provide the exact established correspondence formalism.','Publication 1968.','The theorem is valid. Its printed global cutoff step on scan 53 is false, so the draft proof requires the explicit repair.',[K])
add(254,[124],[53],'1926–1953','ξ^(n−k)T_k est nul sur H^j, j≠k','The one-sided expression L^(n−k)T_k is claimed to have only its degree-k block, and u_kL^(n−k)T_k=π_k. In general it also maps higher degrees of the retained interval to degrees beyond the interval.','false as stated',check+'; H*(P²), n=2,k=1,T₁=π₂: LT₁(h)=h² and ΛLT₁=π₂≠π₁=0.','D','The published intrinsic-projector argument Kleiman 1968 Lemma 3.15 uses two-sided truncation. This exact omitted target cutoff is a new inference of this consolidation, not an earlier-run or second-pass reading.','Mathematical countercheck 2026; established supported version published 1968.','False global formula; refined statement CL-SA19-280 is independently proved.',[K])
add(255,[124],[53,55],'1926–1953;1996–2003','par différence … Cela prouve le lemme','After a correct degree-k projection is obtained, L^j u_(k−2j)L^(n−k+j) restricted to H^k projects onto the sum of string terms with exponent at least j; differences recover individual primitive factors. As a whole-H* formula it requires the degree-k support.','proof sketched','Independent string calculation, proof_checks.md §2.','B','Matched to the supported primitive decomposition in Kleiman 1968 §1 and Proposition 2.9.','Publication 1968.','Correct on H^k, with explicit projector support globally. The preceding cutoff error cannot be carried into this step.',[K])

# Contextual commutative and homological algebra follows.
add(256,[137],[35],'1365–1392','Cohomologie des catégories de Banach par Max Karoubi','Karoubi proposes higher K-groups of suitable Banach categories with K⁰ the split Grothendieck group and periodicity related to Bott. The supplied page begins definitions, not a proof of all periodicity or exactness claims.','programmatic statement in a reused page explicitly attributed to Karoubi','Compared expanded published definitions and periodicity theorems; no independent Bott proof.','B','Exact title is Karoubi 1966, C. R. Acad. Sci. Paris 263, A275–A278; the expanded treatment is Karoubi 1968, Algèbres de Clifford et K-théorie, especially §2.3.','Announcement 1966; expanded publication 1968.','Established under Karoubi’s precise categorical hypotheses. This is contextual material, not a Grothendieck theorem about Jacobians.', ['Karoubi1966','Karoubi1968'])
add(257,[108],[37],'1446–1463','the sheaf Ĩ is injective … all O_X-modules','For a noetherian ring A and prime p, let I=E_A(k(p)). On Spec A the associated sheaf is the point-direct-image sheaf determined by I at p, constant along its closure in the stated sense, and is injective in the category of all O_X-modules.','proved there','Independent exact-stalk/right-adjoint argument in proof_checks.md §9; localization structure checked against Matlis.','E','The cited Matlis 1958 theory supplies the module structure; Stacks sheaf-injective constructions verify the exact adjunction. The exact reused English typescript has not been identified as a publication.','Matlis publication 1958; exact fragment date not determined.','Correct; a vector-space extension argument alone would be insufficient without the O_(X,p)-module adjunction.', ['Matlis1958',S])
add(258,[108],[37,39],'1446–1463;1523–1543','F can be written as a direct sum F=⊕F_α','For a locally noetherian scheme X and quasi-coherent F, injectivity among all O_X-modules is characterized by a direct-sum decomposition into the point-associated indecomposable injective hull sheaves. The supplied page is cut off during the second condition.','stated without proof on the supplied fragment','Used Matlis decomposition locally; no independent global proof claimed.','B','Searched Hartshorne Residues and Duality/Matlis sheaf contexts and current Stacks injective-module sections. The mathematical theorem is standard; exact authorship/edition of the fragment remains unverified.','Module precursor 1958; later duality literature 1966 and subsequent accounts.','Established in the locally noetherian quasi-coherent setting; do not extend to arbitrary ringed spaces or arbitrary non-quasi-coherent sheaves.', ['Matlis1958','Hartshorne1966',S],'medium')
add(259,[107],[39],'1523–1543','We recall the following theorem, due to Matlis','Over a commutative noetherian ring, every injective module decomposes into indecomposable injective hulls E(A/p), with the usual uniqueness of multiplicities. For I=E(k(p)), the annihilator filtration has finite-length layers dual to the local maximal-ideal graded pieces, hence the displayed equal dimensions.','stated with explicit prior attribution','Read Matlis 1958 Theorems 2.5,3.1 and 3.10; the last provides the filtration duality, not an invented attribution.','E','Exactly Matlis, Injective modules over Noetherian rings (1958), especially the classification and Theorem 3.10. The manuscript calls it a recalled theorem.','Received 10 April 1958, revised 9 June 1958; publication 1958.','Established before these pages. Uniqueness is of decomposition type/multiplicities, not canonical summands or canonical hull isomorphisms.', ['Matlis1958'])
add(260,[116],[41],'1586–1613','Ext^i(L,M) is coherent (resp. quasi-coherent)','On a locally noetherian scheme, coherent L admits local finite free resolutions to every finite length. If M is coherent (respectively quasi-coherent), each sheaf Ext^i(L,M) is coherent (respectively quasi-coherent), by the displayed dimension-shifting induction.','proved there','Independent finite-presentation/syzygy induction; proof_checks.md §9.','B','Current Stacks derived-category/coherent-Ext results, especially Lemma 36.11.5, supply the precise modern formulation. The exact reused English sheet remains unidentified.','Standard noetherian homological algebra by the 1960s; exact first publication not assigned.','Correct with noetherian/coherent hypotheses. Global Ext groups need not be finite without properness.',[S])
add(261,[116],[41],'1586–1613','Assume either a) … or b) … RHom … coherent','Let X be locally noetherian, L have coherent cohomology and M∈D⁺ with coherent (respectively quasi-coherent) cohomology. The asserted conclusion for RHom is valid if L∈D⁻, or if L∈D⁺ and the given uniform vanishing of Ext^i(F,M) for all coherent F holds. Dropping both controls is false.','stated with proof left as exercise','Independent finite-diagonal/truncation argument; counterexample over k with zero-differential k in every nonnegative degree gives H⁰RHom=∏_(j≥0)k, not coherent.','B','Stacks Lemma 36.11.5 (tag 0D0C) and finite-injective-dimension Lemma 47.15.2 (0G4H) give the corresponding modern results. No unbounded extrapolation is supported.','Later formal accounts checked as of 2026; exact reused typescript publication not established.','Qualified theorem established. The unbounded extrapolation belongs to the earlier run’s warning, not a claim on the source page.',[S])
add(262,[117],[43],'1706–1716','K … extension séparable de k','In the omitted noetherian local/completed-algebra context of EGA IV 18.11.5, if the closed fibre over the indicated polynomial or power-series base has a unique point and the finite map is unramified there, the residue field extension is separable. An inseparable residue field obstructs the proposed conclusion at the maximal ideal.','proved there','Local fibre criterion for unramified morphisms checked; context and bracket notation left explicit.','A','EGA IV4 Remark18.11.6 is the identified publication locator from the galley and a modern English transcription. Primary publication metadata was opened, but the49.85MB primary PDF exceeded web limits and two downloads stalled. Exact primary passage remains unread, so the publication comparison is a separate lead, not a verified two-endpoint link. The local mathematical obstruction is independently checked.','EGA IV4 publication 1967.','The separability obstruction is correct. Missing surrounding hypotheses prevent treating the sheet as a theorem about arbitrary finitely generated k-algebras.', ['EGAIV4',S])
add(263,[117],[43],'1706–1716','Vider|Virer (ii) sauf raison sérieuse !','For J=ker(A→A_p), A′=A/J has A_p≅A′_(p/J), so the displayed quotient reduction transfers local differential information. The attempted extension of 18.11.5 is incomplete on the sheet and marked for deletion unless a serious reason remains.','withdrawal proposed on the page; proof incomplete','Checked localization identity; no proof claimed for the missing final conclusion.','F','Located the EGA IV4 18.11.6–18.11.7 neighborhood through the supplied galley and an English translation proxy; exact published primary text was unavailable. The supplied fragment ends before its conclusion, and the marginal Vider|Virer reading remains undecided. No verified published counterpart to the deleted(ii) is claimed.','Galley before publication 1967; exact deletion history not established.','Record the abandoned reduction and current reading; do not manufacture a surviving theorem or a reason for the deletion.', ['EGAIV4'],'medium')
add(264,[112],[52],'1883–1925','premier … compris entre Ann(M) et une de ses puissances','For a finitely generated module M generated by q elements over a commutative ring B, Ann_B(M)^q⊂Fitt₀^B(M)⊂Ann_B(M). The historical first Fitting ideal here is modern Fitt₀.','stated without proof','Independent adjugate and determinant proofs in proof_checks.md §9.','E','Stacks §15.8, especially Lemma 15.8.4, confirms the convention and annihilator containment. The source itself refers backward to D. Lazard’s thesis; no claim of Grothendieck originality is made.','Fitting theory predates these pages; earliest exact source not established.','Correct for finite generation, with the ring and indexing fixed.',[S])
add(265,[112],[52],'1883–1925','la différente de Kähler … premier idéal de Fitting','For a finitely presented A-algebra B, D_K=Fitt₀^B(Ω_(B/A)); it cuts out the non-unramified locus set-theoretically and commutes with arbitrary base change. A presentation by B-free modules is required. The literal A^p→A^q presentation does not define that B-ideal.','definition and claims stated; wrong ring in displayed presentation','Presentation/base-change proof and determinant convention checked in proof_checks.md §9.','B','Stacks §49.7, Definition 49.7.1 and Lemmas 49.7.2–49.7.4, provide the exact modern formulation.','Established classical theory; current precise reference checked 2026.','Correct with B-linear presentation. The source ring error is an unresolved ink alarm, not silently changed.',[S])
add(266,[113],[52],'1883–1925','C intersection complète locale sur B … transitivité marche','The note asks for transitivity of differents for A→B→C with a local complete-intersection condition on the second map, but omits finiteness/flatness/generic separability and which different is meant. In a finite flat complete-intersection tower the Jacobian block determinant gives D_(C/A)=D_(C/B)·D_(B/A)C.','stated without full hypotheses',check+'; triangular two-equation Jacobian determinant f_xg_y; this verifies the model, not every omitted generality.','C','Stacks §§49.2,49.9,49.12 identify the canonical module comparison and equality of Kähler/Noether/relative differents under explicit quasi-finite flat lci hypotheses. No unrestricted transitivity theorem is imported into the source.','Classical theory; precise modern statements checked 2026.','Qualified model established; strongest exact source claim remains underdetermined until its Bourbaki context is recovered.',[S],'medium')
add(267,[114],[52],'1883–1925','Dedekind … coïncide … Kähler … anneaux de valuation discrète','For a finite extension of discrete valuation rings with separable fraction-field extension, the trace-defined complementary module and inverse different agree with the Kähler different in the finite flat lci situation. Generic inseparability makes the ordinary trace pairing degenerate and requires another formulation.','stated with references to a missing chapter','Checked trace assumptions and Stacks different comparison; no computation substitutes for finiteness hypotheses.','E','Stacks §§49.8–49.12 gives the trace, relative and Kähler comparison. The source labels this an exposition of an existing theorem (th.3, §2,n°7), not a new result.','Classical different theory before these notes; exact missing edition not identified.','Correct qualified classical result. The bare words DVRs do not supply finiteness or generic separability.',[S])
add(268,[105],[52,54],'1883–1925;1954–1995','R monogène ssi … idéaux maximaux … homogènes','The proposed monogenicity criterion for finite k-algebra R with étale semisimple quotient uses the confirmed word homogènes without an ambient grading. Even replacing it by principaux does not yield a valid unrestricted criterion: over k=F₂, R=k³ is finite étale with principal maximal ideals and is not monogenic. The nonzero discriminant polynomial need not have a k-rational nonvanishing value over finite k.','false as an unrestricted mathematical reconstruction; literal homogènes undefined',check+'; enumerate all eight elements of F₂³, generated ranks≤2, all Vandermonde discriminants zero.','D','Searched primitive-element/finite étale and monogenic algebra references, including Stacks standard étale local structure. None validates the unrestricted sentence; local monogenicity and global monogenicity are different.','Classical primitive-element background; explicit finite-field countercheck 2026.','No ink repair decided. A correct theorem needs field-cardinality or separation conditions and the local principal-maximal-ideal hypothesis stated precisely.',[S])
add(269,[114],[52],'1883–1925','extensions totalement et modérément ramifiées … racine n-ième d’une uniformisante','For a finite totally tamely ramified extension of complete discretely valued fields of ramification index e prime to residue characteristic and unchanged residue field, one may choose uniformizers so that the extension is generated by an e-th root of a suitable base uniformizer. Calling it a cyclic Kummer extension further requires the relevant roots of unity.','stated as an exposition plan','Reviewed uniformizer/unit adjustment via Hensel lifting; no Galois conclusion inferred without μ_e.','E','Searched classical local-field ramification and current Stacks tame DVR discussion; the source explicitly presents this as material to include, not a conjecture.','Classical local-field theory before the manuscript; exact missing Bourbaki edition unestablished.','Established under completeness, total tame ramification and the stated residue-field assumptions.', ['Serre1962',S],'medium')
add(270,[114],[52],'1883–1925','surfaces de Riemann … générateur canonique exp(2iπ/n)','For the local complex cover z↦z^n, the positively oriented loop around the branch value induces the deck transformation z↦exp(2πi/n)z. Its canonicity uses the complex orientation and the chosen local covering interpretation; arbitrary tame inertia over a field has no distinguished generator.','example stated without proof','Direct local analytic monodromy calculation; proof_checks.md §9.','E','Searched the local ramification context; this is classical Riemann-surface monodromy, distinct from choosing compatible roots of unity in arithmetic settings.','Classical before these pages.','Correct in the oriented complex local model; no canonical arithmetic generator is inferred.', ['Serre1962'])
add(271,[115],[54,56],'1954–1995;2004–2050','B_q est normal; Si A est réduite, B est réduite','Étale algebras over a normal ring are normal, locally at the indicated points; étale algebras over a reduced ring are reduced. These are ascent properties, with flatness essential to étaleness.','stated without proof','Compared modern étale ascent results; no independent normality proof claimed.','A','EGA IV4 §18 and current Stacks étale properties are the relevant published framework; exact detached Bourbaki proposition numbering not matched.','EGA IV4 publication 1967; related SGA1 original seminar 1960–1961, publication 1971.','Established for étale morphisms. Unramified alone does not give these conclusions over arbitrary bases.', ['EGAIV4','SGA1',S])
add(272,[115],[54],'1954–1995','améliorations dépendant du main-theorem … prop.6 … Tribu64','The editorial instructions propose unspecified refinements using Zariski’s main theorem and an automorphism-group proposition in a missing Tribu64 text. The source does not state the actual propositions or hypotheses.','programmatic directions; statements absent','No result check claimed.','F','Searched EGA IV §18, SGA1, and the finite/unramified local structure context. The detached proposition numbers do not identify an exact mathematical claim.','No exact fate assignable without the referenced draft.','Retain as editorial open tasks/context, not inferred theorems about all automorphism schemes.', ['EGAIV4','SGA1'],'low')
add(273,[119],[54],'1954–1995','B finie … fibres étales … B étale finie','The page’s c) finite over A with all maximal residue fibres étale is asserted equivalent to finite étale. Without flatness this is false. Take A=k[t], B=A×A/(t): away from t=0 the fibre is k(s), at t=0 it is k×k, all finite étale, but B has nonzero t-torsion and is not A-flat.','false as stated in detached excerpt','Exact algebraic counterexample in proof_checks.md §9; no numerical test needed.','D','Compared Stacks Lemma 29.37.15 (02GU), whose equivalent fibre criterion explicitly retains flatness. Missing ambient flatness in the original Bourbaki draft is possible but not supplied on the page.','Classical corrected criterion; counterexample checked 2026.','False literal detached formulation; refined finite flat/finitely presented criterion CL-SA19-281.',[S])
add(274,[115],[54],'1954–1995','I nilpotent … équivalence de catégories … algèbres étales finies','For a nilpotent ideal I⊂A, reduction modulo I gives an equivalence between finite étale A-algebras and finite étale A/I-algebras; this is an equivalence of categories, not equality of rings or a canonical choice of representatives.','stated as proposed appendix; cites SGA','Read Stacks finite-étale thickening lemma and topological invariance proof; no independent deformation-theory proof claimed.','A','SGA1 exposé I nilpotent invariance is the historical setting; EGA IV4 18.1.2 and Stacks Lemma 58.8.3 (0BQB), Theorem 59.45.2 (04DY), give precise forms.','SGA seminar 1960–1961; EGA publication 1967; SGA1 volume 1971.','Established, including the stronger étale-site invariance under universal homeomorphisms.', ['SGA1','EGAIV4',S])
add(275,[115],[56],'2004–2050','étale = non ramifiée + plate','For a finitely presented A-algebra B, étale is equivalent to flat and having finite separable local residue fibres, equivalently flat and Ω_(B/A)=0. These properties are stable under composition and arbitrary base change, and descend faithfully flatly with the finiteness conditions.','definitions and properties stated','Matched to Stacks étale ring maps and fibre criterion, especially 02GU.','A','EGA IV4 §§17–18 and SGA1 establish this framework; current Stacks §10.143, §10.151 and Lemma 29.37.15 match the exact local hypotheses.','SGA1 lectures 1960–1961; EGA IV4 1967; SGA1 publication 1971.','Established. Finite separability of residue fields alone, without the full local-fibre condition and flatness, is insufficient.', ['EGAIV4','SGA1',S])
add(276,[115],[56],'2004–2050','B étale en q … B̂_q étale finie sur Â_p','For a locally finite-type morphism of noetherian schemes, étaleness at q is equivalent to the induced map of completed local rings being finite étale. The analogous unramified statement has its corresponding completed local criterion.','stated without proof','Read Stacks Lemmas 41.3.4 and 41.11.3: faithful flatness of completions and finiteness of the completed fibre; no numerical computation.','A','EGA IV4/SGA1 local theory; exact current formulation Stacks tag 039M. The completed algebra is finite free as a module, not necessarily a product of base rings without separably closed residue field.','Historical EGA/SGA publications 1967/1971; exact earlier circulation not assigned.','Correct with noetherian and essentially finite-type hypotheses and completed local rings.', ['EGAIV4','SGA1',S])
add(277,[115],[56],'2004–2050','A connexe … A′⊗B … produit de copies de A′','For a connected ring A, a finite étale algebra B has constant finite rank and becomes A′^n after a faithfully flat base extension A→A′. Conversely such a finite product trivialization descends finite étaleness. For a disconnected base the rank may vary and the formulation is piecewise.','stated without proof','Descent and rank qualification checked against finite étale theory; no independent full fpqc descent proof.','A','SGA1 finite étale descent and current Stacks finite étale splitting theory match this statement. The page itself correctly warns about disconnected bases.','SGA1 seminar 1960–1961, publication 1971; EGA IV4 1967.','Established with finite product and faithful flatness; the connectedness caveat is substantive.', ['SGA1','EGAIV4',S])
add(278,[105,115],[54,56],'1954–1995;2004–2050','structure locale … x … F′(x) inversible','Finite or essentially finite-type unramified/étale morphisms admit, around a chosen point and with suitable localizations, a standard monogenic étale description with invertible derivative. The isolated minimal-polynomial wording over a general base needs its missing context. Local standard étaleness does not imply a global monogenic algebra.','stated across an incomplete page boundary','Compared standard étale presentation theorem and F₂³ global obstruction.','A','EGA IV4 §18.4 and current Stacks standard étale local structure provide the precise modern theorem; exact detached proposition n°4 is not identified by its page number.','EGA IV4 publication 1967; SGA1 original lectures earlier.','Established local theorem; no global primitive-element conclusion over arbitrary finite fields.', ['EGAIV4','SGA1',S])
add(279,[122],[14,15,16],'482–649','[earlier run’s reconstructed normalized model]','Reconstruction, not literal source text: on one free integral unit-step string of each length 1,…,n+1, the algebra generated by L and Λ is ∏_(i=0)^n Mat_(n−i+1)(ℤ). With e=1−LΛ, e_i=eΛ^(n−i)L^(n−i)e−eΛ^(n−i+1)L^(n−i+1)e isolates a string bottom and L^b e_iΛ^a are its matrix units.','idea of the earlier run, checked here',check+'; all units checked for n≤5, all-n proof in proof_checks.md §3.','D','Compared the published Lefschetz formalism, but no exact identification of this integral replacement order with the manuscript’s literal exterior-contraction construction was found.','Earlier-run reconstruction; general proof checked 2026.','Established reconstructed algebra. Refines CL-SA19-208 and CL-SA19-209, without amending the transcription.',[K])
add(280,[124],[5,8,11,17,51,53,55],'123–173;234–264;350–392;650–692;1861–1882;1926–2003','[this run’s explicit two-sided support repair]','Assume a finite grading [0,2n] and homogeneous two-sided inverse maps w_k for L^(n−k) on complementary blocks. If outer projectors are known, define T_k to remove them, V_k=T_kL^(n−k)T_k and W_k=T_kw_kT_k. Then W_kV_k=π_k and V_kW_k=π_(2n−k); induction obtains every π_i and supported primitive projectors.','reconstruction of the intended proof, not changed ink','Independent all-degree support proof; exact P² counterexample distinguishes it from CL-SA19-254.','B','Kleiman 1968 Lemma 3.15 confirms the necessity and availability of two-sided truncated blocks; the explicit repair is stated here to make the defective proof reviewable.','Published formal framework 1968; this exact audit repair 2026.','Proved under the displayed hypotheses; refines CL-SA19-203, CL-SA19-206, CL-SA19-212, CL-SA19-254 and CL-SA19-255.',[K])
add(281,[119],[54,56],'1954–1995;2004–2050','[flatness retained in the fibre criterion]','Refinement: if B is a finite, flat, finitely presented A-algebra, then it is finite étale if and only if its residue fibres are finite étale. It suffices to test the maximal fibres in the finite situation; the unramified locus and support of Ω give the local test.','reconstruction retaining the flatness visible on scan 56','Direct use of finite presentation, Ω fibre test and Nakayama; counterexample CL-SA19-273 checks necessity.','B','Stacks Lemma 29.37.15 (02GU) gives the exact flat-plus-fibre equivalence; finite étale is the finite specialization.','Classical EGA/SGA theory; precise current statement checked 2026.','Established; refines the detached false finite-fibre criterion without guessing a missing ambient assumption.',[S])

add(282,[135],[47],'1785–1796','In car.0, A(X) is known for dim X≤4','In the letter’s convention A(X) concerns the Lefschetz isomorphisms on rational algebraic cycle classes, whereas its C(X) concerns algebraicity of the inverse cohomology operators. Over characteristic zero, A(X) holds in dimensions at most four; the first potentially nontrivial critical codimension-two inverse occurs in dimension five. This is not the assertion that the Lefschetz standard conjecture B holds for every fourfold.','stated as a list of known facts','Dimension-by-dimension reduction: outside degree zero/top, dimensions≤4 require only divisor classes and the rational (1,1) theorem; middle degree is tautological. No new proof of a standard conjecture is asserted.','E','Compared Kleiman 1968 §§2.1–2.14 and §3 (Lefschetz/index distinction), the letter’s exact dictionary, and the primary modern standard-conjecture survey in Ancona et al. The low-dimensional cycle statement is classical in characteristic zero; the dimension-five critical problem remains part of the general conjectural picture.','Known to the letter of 4 January 1967; formal published framework 1968.','The source’s low-dimensional assertion is valid with rational cycle classes. Its dim5 phrase is an open frontier in the letter, not a claim that every fivefold is currently unresolved.',[K,'AnconaEtAl2025'],'high for dictionary and classical reduction; medium for every modern special case')
add(283,[135,136],[46,47],'1759–1796','H^(2n−1)(X)→H¹(X) … π₀,π_(2n),π₁,π_(2n−1) … algebraic','For smooth projective geometrically connected X of dimension n, with rational ℓ-adic cohomology and ℓ invertible, the inverse to L^(n−1):H¹(X)→H^(2n−1)(X) is induced by a rational algebraic correspondence via Picard and Albanese varieties. The four outer Künneth projectors π₀,π₁,π_(2n−1),π_(2n) are algebraic. For a general abstract Weil theory, the H¹/Picard comparison must be assumed or proved.','stated as known; Hodge attribution on following page','Read Kleiman 2A9, including assertions5–6 and the Picard/Albanese isogeny proof; compare2A10. No independent construction of the Picard scheme claimed.','B','Kleiman 1968 Theorem2A9 gives the inverse through a Poincaré divisor and an isogeny, with the ℓ-adic comparison in(6). The letter itself calls the result known; this class records its precise subsequent exposition, not priority. The original Hodge1938 surface attribution is separately qualified in CL-SA19-245.','Known on the 1967 letter; precise checked exposition1968.','Established under the stated cohomology hypotheses; does not supply middle projectors or all Lefschetz inverses in arbitrary dimension.',[K,'Hodge1938'])
add(284,[134,129,130],[48,49],'1797–1832','Maybe with some extra care this can be proved too in arbitrary characteristics','The letter asks whether rational characteristic-polynomial coefficients for every algebraic degree-preserving endomorphism can be obtained in arbitrary characteristic, so that existence of algebraic complementary-degree isomorphisms suffices for the Lefschetz inverse conclusion. Over finite fields, Katz–Messing prove the needed rationality (integrality for integral correspondences), independently of the Weil cohomology, for a smooth projective absolutely irreducible variety and correspondences defined over that finite field.','open on the pages; positive-characteristic extension suggested','Katz–Messing Theorem2(1)–(2) and its proof read from primary pp.76–77, with images visually verified. Their projectors are rational polynomials in Frobenius obtained by coprime characteristic polynomials and Cayley–Hamilton. The source’s inverse-polynomial step is independently checked in proof_checks.md §8.','C','Katz–Messing1974 Theorem2 settles the precise finite-field case and hence cases descending with their correspondence to finite fields. It does not by itself cover an arbitrary positive-characteristic field with transcendental moduli, prove numerical=homological equivalence, or establish algebraic Lefschetz inverse cycles universally.','Katz–Messing manuscript received8August1973; journal publication1974. No earlier first-circulation date for their exact theorem established.','Finite-field claim established; the universal general-field extension is not established by the searched sources. The formal conditional Cayley–Hamilton argument remains valid.', ['KatzMessing1974',K,'Jannsen1992'])

